  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 AM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nTODAY - SUNDAY:\n\nA ridge of surface high pressure is sliding through northern\nWisconsin during the current overnight hours, leading to calm to\nlight and variable winds with mostly clear skies aside from some\nhigh-level clouds in east-central Minnesota into northwest\nWisconsin. These conditions are generally favorable for patchy\nfog and/or low stratus development towards the 3-6 AM \ntimeframe, improving by mid-morning should any develop. Areas of\nbest potential for any fog development would be in the Brainerd\nLakes northeast to the Iron Range. \n\nOtherwise, expect any stratus that develops to mix out by mid to\nlate morning, leading to a seasonably mild day with increasing\nsoutheast winds and high temperatures in the mid 40s to low/mid\n50s, warmest in north-central Minnesota. A weak surface trough\nand cold front move NW to SE across the Northland this evening\nand tonight, switching winds to westerly and northwesterly and bring\nsome clouds, but dry low to mid-levels of the atmosphere will \nkeep the frontal passage dry with no precipitation. Lows \ntonight bottom out in the low to mid 30s, though slightly warmer\nnear Lake Superior.\n\nBreezy northwest winds persist throughout much of the day on\nSunday, with some gusts up to 35 mph possible for the North\nShore Sunday morning into early afternoon. Despite the dry\nfrontal passage, mid-level shortwave energy across northern\nMinnesota Sunday morning could lead to a few brief sprinkles or\nshowers in the tip of the Arrowhead (10-30% chances). Little to\nno precipitation accumulation expected from this activity.\nSunday will be another mild temperature day, with highs in the\nmid 40s to low 50s, warmest in east-central Minnesota to\nnorthwest Wisconsin.\n\nNEXT WORK WEEK:\n\nNo significant changes to the forecast for next work week. \nMonday and Tuesday will be seasonably warm with highs in the \nmid/upper 40s to mid 50s and largely dry despite a cold frontal \npassage on Monday due to the better forcing with the system and \nthe main low pressure across northern Manitoba and far northern \nOntario. Global ensembles do show a mix of signal regarding mid-\nlevel troughing and much cooler air aloft sliding southward \nTuesday night through Thursday. The lack of moisture with these \nshortwave troughs keeps precipitation chances limited (patchy \n10-20% chances), but the 850 mb temps dropping to -5 to -10 degC\nrange over a fairly warm Lake Superior water surface (+8 to 10 \ndegC) should allow for some lake-effect precipitation during the\nmid to late work week for portions of the South Shore as the \nshortwave troughs move through. Some light rain and snow mix is \npossible Wednesday, though cooling temperatures Wednesday night \ninto Thursday could transition this precipitation over to \nprimarily light snow. Regardless, snowfall amounts appear \nlimited at this time.",
